//? Title: Second-Order Optimization: Newton and a Cauchy-Point Variant
//? Subtitle: Exact quadratic solves, changing curvature and singular-start controls
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A gradient identifies local slope, while a Hessian also describes how that slope
changes. Newton's method uses this curvature to solve a local quadratic model,
but a full step can be unsafe away from a well-conditioned minimum. We check an
exact quadratic solve, compare the library's Cauchy-point trust-region variant
and L-BFGS on Rosenbrock, and retain singular-start failures and conditioning
sensitivity with independent final-gradient checks.
)
\toc

> The method

Expand f(x+s) approximately as f+g transpose s+.5*s transpose H*s. Stationarity
of that local model gives H*s=-g. With an SPD constant Hessian, the undamped
Newton step reaches the quadratic minimizer in one update. This implementation
instead solves (H+damping*I)*delta=g by partial-pivot elimination and subtracts
delta. Its fixed damping is not an adaptive trust-region mechanism, and there
is no line search to reject an increasing objective.

$$
 s_N=-H^{-1}g,\qquad
 s_\delta=-(H+\delta I)^{-1}g,\qquad
 e_{\mathrm{next}}=\delta(H+\delta I)^{-1}e
 \quad\text{for an SPD quadratic}.
$$
\alttext(The undamped Newton step solves the Hessian system against minus the gradient. Fixed diagonal damping leaves the quadratic error multiplied by delta times the inverse shifted Hessian.)

The last identity proves the damping bias of a single step, with contraction at
most delta/(lambda_min(H)+delta) for nonnegative delta. It requires positive
curvature; shifting an indefinite or singular Hessian by a tiny fixed amount does
not supply a general descent or stability guarantee. Dense factorisation costs
O(p cubed) and stores O(p squared), beyond obtaining the exact Hessian.

The retained TikZ diagram needs LaTeX/TikZ, amsmath, lmodern and dvisvgm to rebuild.
Normal documentation exports use its saved local SVG.

```{bash, file="figures/opt_second_order_mechanism.svg"}
set -eu
mkdir -p build
latex -interaction=batchmode -halt-on-error -output-directory=build diagrams/opt_second_order_mechanism.tikz > build/opt_second_order_mechanism.compile.log 2>&1
dvisvgm --no-fonts --bbox=10 --output=build/opt_second_order_mechanism.svg build/opt_second_order_mechanism.dvi > build/opt_second_order_mechanism.render.log 2>&1
printf 'Rendered the optimization mechanism diagram.\n'
```
// result_begin:
// Rendered the optimization mechanism diagram.
// \image(figures/opt_second_order_mechanism.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Curvature can define a Newton solve or scale a restricted gradient direction.)
\alttext(An exact gradient and Hessian build a local model. A Newton solve or Cauchy ray proposes a displacement; independent loss and gradient checks assess the actual returned point.)

`TrustRegionNewton` here uses only a Cauchy point. Set s=-alpha*g, where
alpha=min(g transpose g/(g transpose H*g),radius/norm(g)) for positive ray curvature;
otherwise use the radius boundary. A strict objective decrease is accepted and
doubles the radius, up to its maximum; failure halves it. There is no predicted
versus actual model-reduction ratio, dogleg step or full trust-region solve.
It stops on gradient norm, tiny radius or budget. Hessian use costs O(p squared)
per step, but the direction remains parallel to the gradient.

> Small worked example

>> A quadratic distinguishes the directions in one step

For f=x0 squared+4*x1 squared at (1,1), g=(2,8) and H=diag(2,8). Undamped Newton
gives (0,0). With radius one, the Cauchy ray uses alpha=1/sqrt(68), since this is
smaller than the free ray minimizer 68/520. Its next point is
(1-2/sqrt(68),1-8/sqrt(68)), with positive residual loss. The contour and actual
native results make the difference visible without claiming a universal ranking.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/opt_second_order_one_step.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace o = dm::optim;
  o::QuadraticObjective f({2, 8}, {0, 0});
  o::NewtonOptions      n;
  n.damping        = 0;
  n.max_iterations = 1;
  o::TrustRegionNewtonOptions t;
  t.max_iterations = 1;
  const auto a     = o::trace_optimize<o::HessianFunction>(f, o::Newton(n), {1, 1}),
             b     = o::trace_optimize<o::HessianFunction>(f, o::TrustRegionNewton(t), {1, 1});
  std::vector<double> x, y;
  for (double angle : dm::seql(0, 2 * std::numbers::pi, 201).values()) {
    x.push_back(std::sqrt(5.) * std::cos(angle));
    y.push_back(std::sqrt(1.25) * std::sin(angle));
  }
  auto plot = dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
                  .line(x, y, "initial loss contour", {130, 130, 130})
                  .line({1, a.coordinates[0]}, {1, a.coordinates[1]}, "Newton", {40, 100, 190})
                  .line({1, b.coordinates[0]}, {1, b.coordinates[1]}, "Cauchy", {190, 100, 35})
                  .points(
                      {1, a.coordinates[0], b.coordinates[0]},
                      {1, a.coordinates[1], b.coordinates[1]},
                      "step endpoints",
                      {30, 30, 30},
                      4)
                  .title("One quadratic step: two directions")
                  .x_label("x0")
                  .y_label("x1")
                  .x_limits(-2.5, 2.5)
                  .y_limits(-1.5, 2.5)
                  .legend(
                      {"loss contour", "Newton", "Cauchy"},
                      {{130, 130, 130}, {40, 100, 190}, {190, 100, 35}});
  plot.size(850, 490).save_svg("build/opt_second_order_one_step.svg");
  std::print(
      "Newton\n\n{}\n\nCauchy-point variant\n\n{}\n", a.summary().to_md(7), b.summary().to_md(7));
}
```
