> Checking changing curvature

>> Rosenbrock with the same declared starting point

Rosenbrock has gradient (2*(x0-1)-400*x0*(x1-x0 squared),200*(x1-x0 squared))
and Hessian with entries H00=2-400*x1+1200*x0 squared, H01=H10=-400*x0, H11=200.
Its zero global minimum is (1,1), but the Hessian can be indefinite elsewhere.
Run Newton (default damping 1e-10, 100-step budget), the Cauchy variant (200), and
L-BFGS (500) from (-1.2,1). Their derivative-request traces are post-run loss checks;
Newton can increase the objective because it has no acceptance safeguard, while
the Cauchy variant's strict-decrease rule can still make slow valley progress.
Budgets, Hessian costs and line-search calls differ, so callback counts are not a
throughput measure.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/opt_second_order_rosenbrock.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace o = dm::optim;
  o::SmoothBenchmark              f("rosenbrock");
  std::vector<o::OptimizationRun> runs;
  runs.push_back(o::trace_optimize<o::HessianFunction>(f, o::Newton(), {-1.2, 1}));
  runs.push_back(o::trace_optimize<o::HessianFunction>(f, o::TrustRegionNewton(), {-1.2, 1}));
  runs.push_back(o::trace_optimize<o::DifferentiableFunction>(f, o::LBFGS(), {-1.2, 1}));
  const std::vector<std::string> labels{"Newton", "Cauchy TR", "L-BFGS"};
  o::optimization_comparison(f, runs, labels, "gradient")
      .title("Rosenbrock: curvature mechanisms")
      .size(850, 490)
      .save_svg("build/opt_second_order_rosenbrock.svg");
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < runs.size(); ++i)
    std::print(
        "{}\n\n{}\n\n{}\n\n",
        labels[i],
        runs[i].summary().to_md(7),
        runs[i].trace.counts().to_md(0));
}
```
