>> A singular start separates an exception from a safe step

At (0,.005), Rosenbrock's Hessian is diag(0,200) and its gradient is (-2,1).
With damping zero, the native pivot threshold rejects a singular solve. With
default damping 1e-10, the first x0 displacement is about two times ten to the ten:
a numerically nonzero pivot permits an enormous step. It need not be a useful
regularisation. Compare six-step runs with small/default and .1 damping against
a six-step radius-one Cauchy run. The singular exception is printed separately;
the finite-budget paths and final checks retain any poor Newton result rather
than calling a non-throwing run successful.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/opt_second_order_singular.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace o = dm::optim;
  o::SmoothBenchmark f("rosenbrock");
  o::NewtonOptions   n;
  n.damping           = 0;
  n.max_iterations    = 6;
  const auto singular = o::trace_optimize<o::HessianFunction>(f, o::Newton(n), {0, .005});
  std::print("Undamped singular control\n\n{}\n\n", singular.summary().to_md(7));
  std::vector<o::OptimizationRun> runs;
  for (double shift : {1e-10, .1}) {
    n.damping = shift;
    runs.push_back(o::trace_optimize<o::HessianFunction>(f, o::Newton(n), {0, .005}));
  }
  o::TrustRegionNewtonOptions t;
  t.max_iterations = 6;
  runs.push_back(o::trace_optimize<o::HessianFunction>(f, o::TrustRegionNewton(t), {0, .005}));
  const std::vector<std::string> labels{"shift 1e-10", "shift .1", "Cauchy TR"};
  o::optimization_comparison(f, runs, labels, "gradient")
      .title("Near-singular curvature: six-step budgets")
      .size(850, 490)
      .save_svg("build/opt_second_order_singular.svg");
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < runs.size(); ++i)
    std::print("{}\n\n{}\n\n", labels[i], runs[i].summary().to_md(7));
}
```
