>> Conditioning affects the Cauchy ray differently from an exact solve

Sweep diagonal Hessians (2,kappa) for kappa=2,20,200,2000 at (1,1). Undamped
Newton still solves each exactly in one update. The default Cauchy variant retains
its 200-step budget and radius adaptation, so its final gradient diagnoses
remaining error. This is a controlled geometry test, not a claim that forming and
solving a dense Hessian is cheaper than requesting a gradient. No dataset is
required for these exact-curvature checks.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/opt_second_order_conditioning.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace o = dm::optim;
  std::vector<double> axis, newton, trust;
  for (double k : {2., 20., 200., 2000.}) {
    o::QuadraticObjective f({2, k}, {0, 0});
    o::NewtonOptions      opt;
    opt.damping        = 0;
    opt.max_iterations = 1;
    const auto a       = o::trace_optimize<o::HessianFunction>(f, o::Newton(opt), {1, 1}),
               b       = o::trace_optimize<o::HessianFunction>(f, o::TrustRegionNewton(), {1, 1});
    axis.push_back(std::log10(k / 2));
    newton.push_back(std::log10(std::max(1e-14, a.gradient_norm)));
    trust.push_back(std::log10(std::max(1e-14, b.gradient_norm)));
    std::print(
        "High curvature {}\n\nNewton\n\n{}\n\nCauchy TR\n\n{}\n\n",
        k,
        a.summary().to_md(7),
        b.summary().to_md(7));
  }
  auto plot = dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
                  .line(axis, newton, "one Newton step", {40, 100, 190})
                  .line(axis, trust, "200-step Cauchy budget", {190, 100, 35})
                  .title("Quadratic conditioning and final gradients")
                  .x_label("log10 condition number")
                  .y_label("log10 final gradient norm (floor 1e-14)")
                  .x_limits(0, 3)
                  .y_limits(-15, 5)
                  .legend({"one Newton step", "Cauchy budget"}, {{40, 100, 190}, {190, 100, 35}});
  plot.size(850, 490).save_svg("build/opt_second_order_conditioning.svg");
}
```
